72K·m/W, at 0% moisture, resulting in current ratings from 518.34 A to 224.21 A. Worst-case con- ditions—high resistivity, increased depth, 1150mm and elevated soil temperature, 28 °C—reduced ampacity by over 45%. Te fndings underscore the need to incorporate site-specifc soil data and worst-case assumptions into cable rating designs to preventthermal degradation. Te developed method ofers a practical, locally optimised alternative for utilities in semiarid regions. Keywords: cable ampacity rating; electrical cable; power cable; soil drying out; soil thermal resistivity; South African conditions 1.Introduction Te novel generation power system requires diverse energy resourcestosupportthereliabilityandsecurityoftheelectric grid [1]. Te electrical power cables, underground and overlines, suitably sustain the integrati"
