lication, and Information & Management. TUURE TUUNANEN is a Senior Lecturer in the Department of Information Systems and Operations Management at the University of Auckland. He holds a D.Sc. (Econ) from the Helsinki School of Economics. His current research interests lie in the areas of IS development methods and processes, requirements engineering, risk management, and convergence of IS and marketing disciplines, speciﬁcally in design of interactive consumer services and products. His research has been published in Information & Management, Journal of Database Management, Journal of Information Technology Theory and Application, Journal of Management Information Systems, and Technology Analysis and Strategic Management. In addition, his work has appea"
A systematic mapping of dynamic thermal rating for underground cables.pdf,5961638,1782589515.5933058,2026-06-27T14:45:15,e56403031210045271a6863ae15564a66381ce833231f55c34d3fedc68830c1c,16,A Systematic Mapping of Dynamic Thermal Rating for Underground Cables,,D:20260128111853+05'30',,20,"Received 18 October 2025, accepted 15 December 2025, date of publication 1 January 2026, date of current version 29 January 2026. Digital Object Identifier 10.1 109/ACCESS.2025.3650354 A Systematic Mapping of Dynamic Thermal Rating for Underground Cables KHAIRUNNISA NORFAEZA M. K. FARIZ 1, NIK HAKIMI NIK ALI 1,2, (Senior Member, IEEE), MOHD IQBAL MOHD NOOR3,4, MOHD NOOR AFIQ BIN RAMLEE5,6, MOHAMMAD A. HAMDAN 7, CHIN-LEONG WOOI 8, (Senior Member, IEEE), AND MOHD AZRAEI PANGAH @ PA’AT9 1Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi MARA, Shah Alam, Selangor 40450, Malaysia 2NANO-Electronic Centre (NET), Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi MARA (UiTM), Shah Alam, Selangor 40450, Malaysia 3Faculty of Business Management, Universiti Teknologi MARA Cawangan Pahang, Kampus Raub, Raub, Pahang 27600, Malaysia 4Institute for Biodiversity and Sustainable Development, Universiti Teknologi MARA, Shah Alam, Selangor 40450, Malaysia 5Tamhidi Centre, Universiti Sains Islam Malaysia, Nilai, Negeri Sembilan 71800, Malaysia 6Faculty of Computing and Multimedia (FCOM), Universiti Poly-Tech Malaysia, Cheras, Kuala Lumpur 56100, Malaysia 7Electrical Engineering Department, Applied Science Private University, Amman 11937, Jordan 8HVTrans, Centre of Excellence for Renewable Energy, Faculty of Electrical Engineering Technology, Universiti Malaysia Perlis, Pauh Putra Campus, Arau, Perlis 02600, Malaysia 9High Voltage Cable Diagnostics Laboratory, Asset Performance Unit, TNB Research Sdn. Bhd., Kajang, Selangor 43000, Malaysia Corresponding author: Nik Hakimi Nik Ali (hakimiali@uitm.edu.my) This work was supported in part by the Ministry of Higher Education (MOHE) through the Fundamental Research Grant Scheme (FRGS) under Grant FRGS/1/2023/TK07/UITM/02/22; and in part by the Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Universiti Teknologi MARA, Shah Alam, Selangor, Malaysia. ABSTRACT Dynamic Thermal Rating (DTR) offers a flexible and adaptive solution to overcome the limitations of static thermal rating in power transmission. This systematic review aims to map and analyze the current state of research on DTR, specifically focusing on underground cable systems. The review contributes to a clearer understanding of DTR’s role in improving the efficiency and resilience of underground cable networks, while also laying the groundwork for future studies to address current limitations. The novelty of this paper lies in its structured synthesis that links modeling techni"
