high voltage cable problems, with pioneering work done prior to the year 2000. At this time it was only possible (and desirable) to apply FEM to a very limited subset of cases, typically only for cables at the highest voltages. Engineers conducting such models were highly skilled and experienced, with analysis often being conducted by research institutes within TSOs or major cable suppliers, using computing hard ware that was state -of- the-art for the time. The software was often highly specialised, even bespoke to the application, making it beyond the reach of the majority of engineers in a standard cable project. The continued growth in interest in the use of FEM for cable ratings led to the publication of an IEC Technical Report in 2003, representing one of the firs"
